---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Tinline — A direct line to your people">
  <main id="main" class="shell">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">PEER-TO-PEER VOICE · FROM OSVAULD</p>
      <h1>A direct line<br />to <em>your people.</em></h1>
      <p class="lead">Your device. Their device. Your conversation.<br />Voice calls without a phone number, account registration or ads.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="#how">How it works ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/osvauld/tinline">Explore the source ↗</a></div>
      <p class="status"><span aria-hidden="true">●</span> Pre-release · Android testing is being prepared</p>
    </section>
    <section id="how" class="section">
      <p class="eyebrow">THREE STEPS. ONE CONNECTION.</p>
      <div class="grid">
        <article><p class="number">01</p><h2>Make it yours.</h2><p>Create an identity on your device. Save your 24-word recovery phrase somewhere private: it is your way back in.</p></article>
        <article><p class="number">02</p><h2>Share a code.</h2><p>Add someone using their QR code or pasted contact code. Compare safety numbers through a trusted channel.</p></article>
        <article><p class="number">03</p><h2>Just call.</h2><p>Calls connect device to device when possible. Encrypted traffic goes through a public iroh relay when a direct path isn't available.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section split">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">PRIVATE DOESN'T MEAN MYSTERIOUS.</p><h2 class="big">Know what<br />you're trusting.</h2></div>
      <div><p>Calls are encrypted between your devices. The app has no advertising, analytics or tracking SDKs. The code is open source under GPL-3.0-or-later.</p><p>Tinline uses public relay and discovery infrastructure. Peers and network providers can see connection metadata. Contacts and call history are currently local files, not vault-encrypted. Tinline is not an anonymity service.</p><a class="text-link" href="/security">Read the security limitations ↗</a></div>
    </section>
    <section class="section release"><p class="eyebrow">A SMALL START. A REAL CONNECTION.</p><h2 class="big">Calling first.</h2><p>Native Android and desktop apps share one Rust core. Public download links will appear here when testing is ready. Chat, files and groups are development plans—not released features.</p><p class="small">Tinline is not an emergency-calling service.</p></section>
  </main>
</Layout>
